export const CLEAN = {
  "baer-heitev": {
    "1#א": "\"Bones.\" Taz wrote and these are his words: since they are not edible they are not included in the prohibition; nevertheless they absorb through cooking, therefore they combine to nullify the prohibition. Mochiach wrote challenging Rama from what Turei Chayyim wrote — that even the earthenware in a vessel requires sixty against it; if so, here too we might say the bones become nevelah. I say Moshe is truth and his Torah is truth — this bone is truly similar to a metal vessel, for we do not say chein nafsho on it because it has a remedy through hagalah; so too with bone, as explained in Orach Chayyim siman 451 that bone vessels are permitted through hagalah, therefore chein nafsho does not apply here, end of his words. And Ran in chapter 5 of Hullin wrote that soft bones combine with the issur because of the marrow within them and because of their softness moisture emerges; but dry bones or egg shells and the like — even those of issur combine to nullify the issur; and so is the view of Issur Veheter and Bach:",
    "1#ב": "\"The pot.\" Meaning: that pot in which one is now cooking issur and heter:",
    "1#ג": "\"They are stringent.\" And nevertheless, even for the issur they do not combine; but permitted bones combine with the heter. Shach:",
    "2#_": "\"That they absorbed.\" And sixty against the nevelah meat does not suffice, lest the meat diminished in its cooking and entered into the bones — so it is in Tur:",
    "3#_": "\"The absorbed piece.\" Explanation: a piece that absorbed from issur that fell into heter, and there is not sixty in the heter to nullify the issur except through combining the piece — the piece too combines to nullify; however the piece itself is forbidden, as the Mechaber wrote end of siman 106; but we do not practice thus for us — rather sixty is required against the entire piece, and even so the piece itself is forbidden:",
    "4#א": "\"And it diminished.\" Maharshal wrote: it appears even where we saw the issur fall and it did not diminish by one measure together with the heter — nevertheless one does not estimate what the pot absorbed, for one cannot stand on the root of the matter and does not know how much the pot absorbed and how many times spoilage will come from this; rather one always estimates as it appears before us. And Taz wrote: according to this one may not rule leniently in what Shulchan Aruch wrote in this seif, and one estimates with a good estimate, etc.:",
    "4#ב": "\"That it absorbed.\" Taz wrote: some say — perhaps at the fall of the issur the issur was very large and now it contracted; for we do not establish issur presumptively; however I already wrote above that one should not be lenient in this even in min b'mino:",
    "5#א": "\"L'chatchila.\" Shach wrote: some say it is forbidden to nullify it d'oraisa; however b'dieved all agree its law is as the Mechaber wrote afterward, end of his words. And Taz wrote in siman 84 seif 13 it is explained that if his intent is only for something else, such as to fix honey, it is permitted; and in siman 137 seif 2 I wrote that this is specifically if it cannot be done otherwise:",
    "5#ב": "\"He transgressed.\" Meaning: that he threw a small amount of issur into abundant heter that has sixty against it, or that it fell by itself when there was not sixty in the heter but he increased afterward:",
    "5#ג": "\"For his sake.\" For if you permitted one for whom it was nullified on his behalf, we are concerned lest he come to say to a non-Jew or a servant to nullify it for him — therefore we penalize him; but if it was not his and he also did not intend to nullify for himself, it is permitted for him, for his bad deed does not help when the vessel is ben yomo. And Rashal wrote: specifically when this one knew it was nullified for him it is pleasing to him even if he did not command him to nullify it; but if he did not know from him, it is permitted for him, for behold he is like a shogeg; and where it is forbidden for him it is forbidden also for the members of his household. And Taz wrote: if he erred in law and thought it permitted to nullify the issur, he is called a shogeg; and proof from Shabbat chapter Kol Gadol in the Mishnah — if he forgot there is a melachah prohibition here, even though he knows today is Shabbat, he is called a shogeg; so too here; and so it is explicit in Tosafot in Bekhorot daf 23 s.v. savar, etc.:",
    "5#ד": "\"To sell it.\" Shach wrote: and it appears specifically when an Israelite buys at a higher price from a non-Jew — then it is forbidden; for otherwise there is no reason to forbid selling to an Israelite, for his bad deed does not help:",
    "5#ה": "\"Nevelah.\" Shach wrote: at first glance it is difficult — what relevance does this have to chein nafsho? For even according to the one who says we do not say chein nafsho in other issurim — nevertheless a piece that absorbed issur and permitted food was added to it afterward — the piece itself remained in its issur, as all the poskim wrote end of siman 110, that the issur in it is not entirely expelled; rather the Rav comes to inform us that a piece that absorbed issur and permitted food was added afterward — even the addition is forbidden because there is not sixty against the entire piece; but if he increased upon it b'shogeg against all that became nevelah, even in basar b'chalav wet in wet everything is permitted; but in a piece that absorbed issur, even if he increased sixty against the entire piece, the piece itself remained in its issur. And he further wrote: from the words of Beit Yosef it appears that even in basar b'chalav we do not say chein nafsho wet in wet, and he disagrees with him — see there — who left the words of Rama in need of study:",
    "5#ו": "\"They practiced.\" Taz wrote: the reason we need not be concerned lest it was added after it became nevelah — for we do not establish issur presumptively; however if it is known to the teacher that it was added but it is not known when — in this certainly one must investigate afterward when it was added, as appears to me for halachah, end of his words. And Shach wrote: even according to the one who says wet in wet becomes nevelah, and likewise a piece that absorbed issur — for all of them they did not practice investigating, for we do not establish issur; however what the Rav wrote to distinguish between whether the mixture was known or not, and even in chein nafsho there is this distinction — Shach disagrees with him, for according to the one who says chein nafsho, if so immediately when there was not sixty it imparted taste — what difference whether it was known or not? And this is proven also from siman 92 regarding a spoon stuck in — a milking spoon in a meat pot twice, and it was not known in between, that sixty twice is required, because the first time the entire spoon became nevelah; and so several other proofs — see there:",
    "6#א": "\"He increases.\" Shach challenged: in Orach Chayyim siman 677 the Mechaber wrote that the oil in a Chanukah lamp that became mixed with other oil and does not have sixty to nullify — some say one may not add to it to nullify, end of his words; and behold Chanukah is only d'rabbanan — it is forced to distinguish, for there since it was designated for its mitzvah it is stricter, and requires study, end of his words. (And Magen Avraham wants to resolve there that Chanukah is a matter that has a permitted use next year, and I cannot understand his words — for if so even in a thousand it would not be nullified; and why did the Mechaber write there \"and there is not sixty to nullify it\"):",
    "6#ב": "\"To change.\" Taz wrote: and some say regarding branches that fell from a palm tree on Yom Tov into an oven — that one increases upon them prepared branches and nullifies them, for there the issur is light and one does not benefit from them until the time of their removal from the world, and then there is no substance in the issur. And Maharshal wrote: that which we say there the reason of light issur is specifically because it is davar sheyeish lo matirin; but in davar she'eino lo matirin, in all d'rabbanan issurim one increases and nullifies — therefore he ruled b'dieved even if he intended to nullify them and did not know it is forbidden to do so, it is permitted, end of his words; and I proved above that if he did not know there is issur in the matter, it is permitted in all nullifications, end of his words:",
    "6#ג": "\"Dry.\" And Shach wrote: it appears to me to permit in great loss if it is dry in dry and min b'mino and it became known in between — see there, where he brings several proofs for his words; and all this if they have one taste; but two issurim that differ in taste — then each nullifies its fellow — see above siman 98. And Taz wrote: and some say regarding inserting a spoon twice in siman 94 — for here there is a new issur:",
    "6#ד": "\"In meat.\" Shach wrote: and some say it is obvious — for every issur nullified in sixty is to be explained because in siman 298 it will be explained that regarding heter in heter nullification does not apply; it teaches us that here we do not say so — see there; and Turei Chayyim wrote that even l'chatchila it is permitted to put the water in a meat pot since it was already nullified:",
    "7#_": "\"To use.\" And Taz disagrees with this law and brings several proofs that we do not say so except where it also spoils; and Bach and Levush ruled thus — see there; and see siman 122 seif 5:"
  },
  "beer-hagolah": {
    "1#א": "Tur in the name of Rabbeinu Shimshon, and the Rosh agreed, and so too Rashba from Yerushalmi chapter 1 of Orlah:",
    "1#ב": "There; and so wrote Rashba, and so too Ran — meaning the reason that bones in the foreleg do not combine to permit non-sacred meat, daf 98:",
    "1#ג": "There:",
    "2#_": "There, from the words of Rabbeinu Shimshon in chapter 5 of Terumot and Rosh in chapter 7 of Hullin:",
    "3#_": "Rashba in responsum She'elot siman 464, and Rabbeinu Efrayim:",
    "4#א": "The statement of Rabbi Chanina there daf 97, and the first version is for stringency as Rashi explained, and so too the Rosh there and Shulchan Aruch:",
    "4#ב": "Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba, and wrote: because in not its species we hold that a kezayit is in ke'dei achilat pras d'oraisa, and it is possible even up to sixty according to the one who says ta'am k'ikar d'oraisa, like Rabbenu Chananel and those who follow his approach:",
    "4#ג": "There, and like Rambam in chapter 15 of Forbidden Foods:",
    "5#א": "Egg daf 4 from Mishnah 9 chapter 5 of Terumot:",
    "5#ב": "There:",
    "5#ג": "Tur in the name of Rambam chapter 15 of Forbidden Foods, from the law of one who cooks on Shabbat, as I noted in Tur Orach Chayyim siman 318 (and Rashba in the name of Rambam and Raavad):",
    "5#ד": "There, from the words of Rashba:",
    "6#א": "Tur in the name of Rashba (and according to what is brought in Beit Yosef):",
    "6#ב": "There, the statement of Rav Matna, egg daf 4:",
    "7#_": "Rivash siman 249 in the name of Raavad; and such things Tur wrote in siman 122 in the name of Rashba, and the Mechaber wrote them there as well:"
  },
  "beur-hagra": {
    "1#א": "\"A piece, etc.\" Yerushalmi chapter 5 of Terumot — terumah waving does not combine with terumah to forbid on non-sacred produce, but non-sacred waving combines with non-sacred produce to raise the terumah; and in chapter 1 of Orlah and chapter 6 of Nazir, R' Avahu in the name of R' Yochanan — all issurim are estimated as if they were in a shadow and absorbed: what is the case? Here one says noten ta'am one in a hundred, and here one says noten ta'am one in sixty. According to the one who says in sixty, you do not make the foreleg one part in a hundred for the ram; according to the one who says in a hundred, you make the foreleg one part in a hundred for the ram; according to R' Yehudah in a hundred, you remove the bones from the foreleg; and according to the one who says in sixty, you do not remove the bones from the foreleg. And just as you remove the bones from the foreleg, so you remove them from the ram — you cannot, for it teaches: terumah waving does not combine with terumah to forbid on non-sacred produce, but non-sacred waving combines with non-sacred produce to raise the terumah. Rav Beivai asked: does terumah waving combine with non-sacred produce to raise the terumah? From what R' Huna said — issur shells combine with heter — this says terumah waving combines with non-sacred produce to raise the terumah. And in chapter 8 of Terumot, egg in sixty — R' Chanina: the vegetable and the shell combine; R' Zeira: and its issur from within it — meaning we estimate only the food in the egg. R' Huna said issur shells raise the heter; and the Yerushalmi above disagrees with that which there says according to the one who says in sixty, foreleg bones combine with issur, and all the more so they do not combine with heter; and the sugya in chapter 5 of Hullin 98b disagrees further — according to the one who says in a hundred, even heter shells do not combine with heter, as it says meat with meat, etc. And it appears the Yerushalmi \"it appears to me\" and \"just as you find,\" etc. — rather one may say it is not specifically so, and since it mentioned meat in the foreleg it also mentioned meat in the ram; in any event we learn from all the sugyot that heter bones combine with heter, and all the more so according to the one who says in sixty, as written in the Gemara meat and bones with meat, etc.; but according to the one who says in sixty, whether in the Gemara sugya or the Yerushalmi sugya, it appears issur bones combine with issur and all the more so they do not combine with heter; and it appears it disagrees with that of chapter 8 of Terumot, as appears to me. And Ran wrote this case is different — bones in the foreleg combine with issur because of the marrow within them and because of their softness moisture emerges; but dry bones are like shells in chapter 8 of Terumot, which are no better than gid — according to the one who says there is not noten ta'am in gid, it does not forbid; and as written in chapter 11 of Terumot, consecrated bones at the time, etc. R' Avahu in the name of R' Yochanan at the heads of the wings and the cartilage — this is the Mishnah; and so in the Gemara of Pesachim chapter 3 — if we say there is no marrow in them, etc.; and in this they disagree — according to the one who says in a hundred, even those that have marrow do not combine with issur, and we hold like the one who says in sixty; and this is what the Yerushalmi \"it appears to me\" and \"just as you,\" etc. — you cannot, for it teaches \"there is not,\" and it appears that baraita refutes according to the one who says in sixty — but it is not so, for even according to the one who says in sixty he agrees regarding waving and shells; rather according to the one who says in a hundred, even those that have marrow are like waving; and this is what it says \"there is not,\" etc.; and this is what Shulchan Aruch wrote \"but the marrow,\" etc.; and Shach too regarding thin bones, as appears to me from the Yerushalmi \"it appears to me\" at the heads of the wings, etc.:",
    "1#ג": "\"And some are stringent, etc.\" So too Rosh in chapter 5 of Hullin — from the baraita \"there is no terumah waving,\" etc., it appears neither for issur nor for heter; and further it says \"but non-sacred waving,\" etc., it appears but not terumah; and so it appears from the Yerushalmi \"it appears to me\" that says according to the one who says in a hundred \"you remove,\" etc., it appears it does not combine neither for issur nor for heter; and we still do not know from the plain meaning of Rav Beivai's question, and it only challenges there regarding ram bones; and \"just as,\" etc.; and it is found he estimated a hundred without the foreleg bone — if so, even for the conclusion, therefore it does not combine. But one may say he holds so, but for the conclusion a hundred is with the bone; and this is what it says \"for such is essential:\"",
    "2#_": "\"In what case is this stated, etc.\" — it is no worse than a pot, as we say in chapter 4 of Hullin (96b), chapter 5 of Hullin, and chapter 25 — a pot in which one cooked, etc., in noten ta'am — see there.\"",
    "4#_": "\"And we estimate the heter, etc.\" \"This applies, etc.\" — per Rashi there s.v. b'mai, etc.; and the essence of the matter, etc.; meaning if we did not see the issur at the time it fell, and R' Chanina deals with where we saw the issur at the time it fell and it is now as it was and did not contract and the heter contracted. And Rashba wrote: and it appears from his words that in such a case even what was entirely consumed by fire also combines; and it appears to me — what concern is it to us what was at first, now it diminished and the issur returns and forbids; and as written (there) \"and the piece itself is forbidden\" if it fell, etc.; and so from Rambam's words that wrote in what the pot absorbed and stands on the walls, etc.; and we estimate, etc., as written in Shulchan Aruch; and proof from what he wrote end of chapter 5 of Terumot \"just as the non-sacred diminished,\" etc., it appears therefore the terumah did not diminish is forbidden; and he further wrote: some say the first version in the pot itself means what is now in the pot and not what the pot absorbed, and the latter version even in what it absorbed; and they ruled like the latter version of Mar bar Rav Ashi, Hullin 98a — he holds accordingly and they did not challenge him except \"are you saying,\" etc., and without knowing the issur measure, as appears to me; and he wrote: and it is reasonable specifically min b'mino; but in not its species the halachah is like the first version for stringency, for it is a safek d'oraisa; and one must say he holds milk and meat are min b'mino — therefore Mar bar Rav Ashi was lenient; and see siman 98 seif 1, see there [and behold Rambam and Rashi are like the latter version of absorbed pot, except for Rashi if we did not see it at the fall we estimate as it is without the pot; and so Rashba holds in min b'mino; and as he wrote \"are you saying issur,\" etc.; and as written the halachah is like the latter version of Mar bar Rav Ashi, etc.; and they did not challenge, etc., except in not its species it is like the first version; and some say meaning even if we saw it at the fall and it did not contract — and this is the distinction between the first and latter versions; and so in Tur \"and we estimate the heter,\" etc., that also regarding issur, etc.; and Rashba, etc.; and Shulchan Aruch's words are not precise — he began in the language of Tur and concluded \"this applies in not its species,\" etc.]:",
    "5#א": "\"And even if it fell, etc.\" Mishnah end of chapter 5 of Terumot, and afterward it fell there non-sacred, etc.; and Yerushalmi there R' Avahu in the name of R' Yochanan — all issurim that one increased upon them b'shogeg are permitted, b'mezid forbidden; and it is not the Mishnah — shogeg permitted, mezid forbidden — the Mishnah comes regarding terumah: come and hear, even all other matters:\"",
    "5#ג": "\"For the one who nullifies, etc.\" Like one who cooks on Shabbat — according to R' Yosi we hold like both of them because of a penalty, as written in Gittin there 53, and Rami R' Yosi against R' Yosi from what the baraita teaches \"one who cooks,\" etc., for he did not penalize:\"",
    "5#ד": "\"And likewise for one, etc.\" As written in chapter 3 of Yevamot (25a) \"one who comes for this Israelite,\" etc.; and in Bava Metzia 90b \"he sent them a trick,\" etc.; \"obviously our grown son,\" etc.:\"",
    "5#ה": "\"And they are forbidden to sell it, etc.\" As it appears there they sell for slaughter, and even for the conclusion there because otherwise he could have benefited from it; and per Rashi there s.v. dayan, etc.; unlike here; and as written end of chapter 8 of Sheviit \"they sell to its eaters and its money,\" etc.:\"",
    "5#ו": "\"And specifically, etc.\" As written above siman 92, from which it is proven that in dry in dry we do not say chein nafsho, and so wet in wet in other issurim; and as written in chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah (73b) \"two cups,\" etc.:\"",
    "1#ב": "\"And the body of the pot, etc.\" Chapter 5 of Hullin 97b — R' Chanina: when they estimate, etc.; and we hold not like the first version, as Rashi wrote, and all the more so according to Rashba's explanation, as written in s.v., see there:",
    "5#ב": "\"He transgressed, etc.\" Mishnah and the Yerushalmi mentioned, and Gemara Gittin 54b, and like R' Yosi that the halachah is as he against his colleagues, as written in chapter 4 of Eruvin (46b):\"",
    "5#ז": "\"And some say even, etc.\" He learned from what he wrote in chapter 5 of Terumot \"and he did not manage to raise,\" etc.; and they said in Yerushalmi there Rashba — its knowledge sanctifies it, and the Sages say its raising sanctifies it; and even the Sages explain only regarding terumah that requires raising, and the same applies for stringency; and as written in Zevachim 104a \"and there is found tereifah,\" etc.; and per Rashi there s.v. ratzeh; and as Rosh wrote in chapter 5 of Hullin. But in truth it is not comparable there; and two say b'mezid forbidden and it cannot be except when known; and per Rashi in Gittin 54b s.v. ya'al, etc.; and kli sheni when not known:\"",
    "6#א": "\"A d'rabbanan issur, etc.\" For they did not say in the first chapter of Yom Tov (4b) \"branches that fell,\" etc., except that they fell into branches; and as Sifrei \"he adds and increases,\" etc.; and even though they said in chapter 4 of Bekhorot terumat chutz la'aretz Rabah, etc., they said only regarding terumat chutz la'aretz which is lenient and nullified in the majority — unlike other d'rabbanan issurim as written above siman 98 (in s.k. 30); and Tosafot Pesachim 30a s.v. leyashinehu, etc. (Extract) issur, etc. — but, etc., as written in Yerushalmi chapter 2 of Kilayim and Rash brought there \"how does one diminish or add to a se'ah,\" etc. — see there [and certainly no worse than other d'rabbanan]; and it says there \"if to mix, even kli sheni forbidden\" — evidently to mix even in d'rabbanan forbidden; on the surface of not intending to plant; and see siman 296 seif 5 and s.k. 7 (end of extract).",
    "6#ב": "\"And some say there is not, etc.\" What he wrote there \"but in d'rabbanan one nullifies\" — that is like the conclusion there where the issur is light; otherwise igulei develah there he would add to it; and so half a zayit of tallow in chapter 5 of Hullin; and Tosafot there s.v. vetanan, etc.:\"",
    "6#ג": "\"Issur that was nullified, etc.\" As written in Zevachim 31a \"Vayikatz kishan hapigul,\" and even the one who permits there because of eruv machshavot; and in Bekhorot 22a R' Yosi says only, etc.; and even Abaye who disagrees agrees that mum'ah removes tumah, as written there from the Mishnah of chapter 5 of Terumot; and we say (s.k. 7 of Kilayim and Shulchan Aruch) \"one who moves a perforated pot,\" etc.; and we say (Bava Kamma 100 and Shulchan Aruch) \"it breached and some say a ditch,\" etc.; and we do not say first-first nullified; and in chapter 6 of Shabbat (seif 5b) lest the drippings increase, etc.; and even for leniency we say it returns and becomes forbidden, as written end of chapter 7 of Nedarim \"growths of heter raise the issur\" and the entire sugya there; and kli sheni for stringency as written there; and perhaps for stringency this case is different; and in end of chapter 5 of Terumot \"and if it is known the wheat is non-sacred,\" etc.; and there se'ah, etc.; and afterward it fell there, etc.; and in Yerushalmi there \"all issurim that one increased upon them,\" etc.; and in chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah (73b) \"two cups,\" etc.; and in Yerushalmi chapter 2 of Orlah \"terumah grits that were cooked with non-sacred lentils and there is not noten ta'am — he increased upon them terumah grits not its species to forbid\" — the large amount must not exceed two hundred, as you say in two hundred you see the heter as it is, and that issur if there is noten ta'am in it is forbidden, and if not permitted; this that it says \"he increased upon them terumah grits forbidden\" — and that which we say in chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah (73a) \"first-first nullified\" — per Tosafot there; and in Bekhorot there and in chapter Ha'Orlah (chapter 2b) \"and other places\" — that is regarding issur mashahu, but in noten ta'am it returns and becomes forbidden because the taste is recognizable in them; and in Avodah Zarah chapter 5 (73a) \"first-first nullified\" — per Tosafot there; and in Bekhorot there and Orlah chapter 2 \"terumah came up,\" etc.; and terumah came up terumah in not noten ta'am; and as written there R' Acha bar Acha — they combine in noten ta'am but not to forbid; and in chapter 8 of Terumot \"and he did not manage to raise,\" etc.; and in Yerushalmi there Rashba — its knowledge sanctifies it, and the Sages say its raising sanctifies it; and this is what it says \"only,\" etc. — investigate all the \"it appears to me\"; but Rambam in chapter 13 of Terumah ruled its knowledge sanctifies it; and as written in Tosefta chapter 6 of Terumot as the Mishnah \"it appears to me\" — Raavad explains the words of the first Tanna. And even though the essential view is like Raavad and Raavash as his father holds, as written in Yerushalmi \"it appears to me,\" and this is what it says \"since he had to raise it\"; and as written in Pesachim 13b \"and Raavash as his father,\" etc. — nevertheless even the Sages explain only regarding terumah that requires raising and is not like moram; but in other issurim they agree, as written in Tosefta there \"se'ah of orlah that fell to two hundred — he knew and afterward another fell, it is permitted,\" etc., until \"terumah increases over permitted\"; and as written regarding terumah that was raised, as in the Mishnah there \"he raised and another fell,\" etc.; evidently specifically regarding terumah raising is required, and in other issurim through knowledge; and in such a case we say in Tosefta chapter 8 \"terumah leaven that fell into dough and he raised it and afterward it leavened — permitted; sheviit leaven that fell into dough — he knew and afterward it leavened — permitted\"; and in Yerushalmi chapter 2 of Orlah the halachah 2 — plainly one's knowledge permits it; another's knowledge — what will you permit; doubtful knowledge, etc.; and in Zevachim 104a \"and there is found tereifah,\" etc., as appears to me; and so Mordechai in chapter 9 of Bava Kamma on what we say there \"it breached and some say a ditch,\" etc., and \"one who moves a pot\" — deals with when not known, for otherwise it is difficult from chapter 8 of Avodah Zarah where we say first-first nullified; and it cannot be said as Tosafot there, for those too are in two hundred. Nevertheless in the majority knowledge does not help, as written in Tosefta \"it appears to me\" until \"terumah increases,\" etc.; and so raising in terumah as in the Mishnah \"it appears to me\" until \"terumah increases,\" etc.; and so they said in chapter Ha'Orlah (chapter 2b) \"he placed se'ah,\" etc., until the majority; and Tosafot there s.v. R' Yochanan, etc.; and the general principle — in d'oraisa we forbid returns-and-stirs even with knowledge in other issurim, and raising in terumah as appears to me; and as written in Yerushalmi chapter 5 of Terumot as the Mishnah \"it appears to me\" until \"he increases,\" etc. — R' Yosi says this teaches that a matter nullified by Torah law removes its species to forbid; and so dry in dry; and so wet in wet not its species until a portion of ta'am k'ikar d'oraisa; and its species in the majority; but in sixty which is d'rabbanan it does not return and stir if he knew; and so all that stand in a hundred or two hundred or in mashahu — none return and stir since it is d'rabbanan; and as written in Gittin 54b (and see siman 101 s.k. 6) \"and so in terumah and orlah even though we derive from verses — it is asmakhta mere\"; and proof Tosafot Bava Metzia 33a s.v. ve'oleh, etc.; and proof that in chapter kli sheni he derives from kal va'chomer from orlah and does not challenge since it requires one and two hundred; and so in chapter Ha'Orlah he counts michpeza and does not count what stands in a hundred (and Tosafot there bring two more proofs that are not necessary); and this is what it says \"terumah came up,\" etc., \"orlah came up,\" etc.; and in chapter 5 of Avodah Zarah first-first nullified, and even in less than sixty since it is its species; and as written in Tosefta \"it appears to me\" until \"it increases,\" etc.; and all deals with without knowledge — in all the measures of a hundred, two hundred, mashahu, and sixty; and with this everything comes out correctly; and those of \"growths of heter,\" etc., \"it breached and some say a ditch,\" etc., and \"one who moves\" — Mordechai without knowledge, for all the Mishnayot stam are with knowledge; except growths this case is different since automatically it increases like one batch; therefore we find issur only in growths; and Tosafot explain since he ruled like Beit Hillel:",
    "6#ד": "\"A kezayit of milk, etc.\" As written in chapter 5 of Terumot \"she raised it and it fell to another place,\" etc.; and the Sages say there is no medameh except, etc. — meaning and permitted entirely, not like Rambam's explanation there which is astonishing and they already challenged him; and even l'chatchila permitted, as written in chapter 9 of Kilayim \"camel wool and sheep wool that one carded,\" etc.:\"",
    "7#א": "\"If a small amount was absorbed, etc.\" As written in Avodah Zarah 33b \"it was asked of them,\" etc.; and Tosafot s.v. shera, etc.; and Rashba wrote because the vessel's use is cold and nothing significant is absorbed except a small amount, and they use the vessel abundantly and it cannot come to noten ta'am; but forbidden wine that is in mashahu — see there 73a in the Mishnah; and with this the difficulty of Tosafot Pesachim 30a s.v. leyashinehu is resolved — say, etc., that even though chametz use is kli sheni, nevertheless use is abundant except the chametz is small and it cannot all be nullified since it is of heter, as Tosafot end of Avodah Zarah; and as written above siman 94 seif 6; and this is what it says regarding the pot — that of Pesachim is either jugs, as appears to me, not as Tosafot wrote in Pesachim there and R' Yosef's explanation that in Pesachim chapter 5 (46a) in the Mishnah \"how does one separate challah,\" etc.; and even in chutz la'aretz as Rif and poskim wrote — and if so, behold it would be fit even for a tamei kohen through nullification in the majority; and Ran (end of extract). If absorbed, etc. — but, etc., from chapter 11 of Terumot that even visible issur in a small amount is permitted — he pours from vessel to vessel, etc.; \"a barrel that was spilled — one need not sit and wipe,\" etc.; and so a lamp, etc.; not like Rambam who holds it is from the leniencies of terumah; and see siman 122 (s.k. 15) (end of extract):",
    "7#ב": "\"But if, etc.\" As written in Pesachim there — a decree lest he come, etc.; evidently if by way of using what forbids it is forbidden to all; and greater than this they said end of Avodah Zarah that a pot that is not ben yomo is forbidden, etc.; and kli sheni in that same vessel; and some say regarding jugs as written there — it is incidental mere and comparable to first use that one's way is to use abundantly — lest he use a small amount:\""
  },
  "kaf-hachayim": {
    "1#_": "(a) [Seif alef] A piece of nevelah that has meat and bones, etc. — issur bones combine with heter to nullify the issur, etc.; because they are not edible they are not included in the prohibition; nevertheless they absorb through cooking, therefore they combine to nullify the issur because the issur spreads also into them and there is also moisture in them to mix the issur that absorbs from the forbidden meat. Taz s.k. 1. Chokhmat Adam or. 1.",
    "2#_": "(b) And however — soft bones combine with issur because of marrow within them and because of their softness moisture emerges. Ran chapter 5 of Hullin. And Beit Yosef ben yomo. Shach s.k. 1. Pri Chadash end of or. 1 Lechem HaPanim or. 1 Binyamin Zeev or. 1 Kereti or. 5 Minchat Yitzchak on Turei Chayyim general chapter 5 or. 50 Chokhmat Adam there. Orach Chayyim or. 1 — however Tur's view is not to disagree, for bones always combine with heter; only the marrow by itself combines with issur. Minchat Yitzchak there. And so one must say for Shulchan Aruch who cited Tur's language. And in Peirush HaMishnayot he is lenient in not its species and in its species he is lenient even in slight loss one does not rebuke him. Shafan Da'at or. 1 Zivchei Tzedek or. 2 — and soft bones that have marrow, that is all that after drying there appear hollow holes known lest after the marrow dried it became so.",
    "3#_": "(c) And even — a limb from a living animal that fell into heter, bones in it combine with heter even though in eiver min hachai the bone combines to a kezayit — it is a novelty, investigate with your eyes regarding its emission. Sixty — Teivat Gimma parashat Vayera or. 9 Peirush Torah end of or. 1 Zivchei Tzedek or. 7.",
    "4#_": "(d) There. But the marrow in bones, etc. — meaning even if the bone is sealed, because the bone is spongy (from the word sponge) and emits the taste of the marrow within it; and as Tur wrote and the cloth above siman 91 in the name of Rashba regarding what was salted — see there. Shulchan Gavoah part 3 investigation below or. 9.",
    "5#_": "(e) There. And the body of the pot does not combine, etc. — meaning that pot in which one cooks now issur and heter; but a plain pot that was already forbidden and afterward one cooks heter in it — the body of the pot certainly combines with issur. Taz s.k. 2. Chokhmat Adam or. 2 Mishnat Zekenim or. 2.",
    "6#_": "(f) There in the Hagahah. And some are stringent not to combine issur bones with heter to nullify. And also not for issur; but heter bones combine with heter. Shach s.k. 2. Pri Chadash or. 2 Lechem HaPanim or. 2 Kereti or. 4 Chokhmat Adam or. 3 Shafan Da'at or. 2.",
    "7#_": "(g) And so — egg shells and pottery shards placed in a pot do not combine neither for issur nor for heter. Chokhmat Adam there. And so in his explanation there end of or. 2 — egg shells and pottery shards placed in a pot, since they have no moisture they do not combine; only according to Mechaber seif 4 that we estimate in what the pot absorbed in its species, so too we estimate in what the shells absorbed; but we (meaning Ashkenazim) do not practice so, end of his words. And it appears that even for Shulchan Aruch that shells combine with heter to nullify — this is in its species where it is nullified in the majority; but in not its species they do not combine neither for issur nor for heter."
  },
  "kereti": {
    "5#ב": "Forbidden to sell it; and Shach's view — if he does not sell at higher price to an Israelite, permitted; and see Peleti.",
    "5#ד": "Before it was known; and Issur Veheter's view — even chein nafsho we do not say before the mixture was known; Shach challenged and wrote therefore Rama omitted it; and see what I wrote in Peleti — also Issur Veheter's view in other issurim and not in basar b'chalav; and I settled Shach's difficulties on Issur Veheter:",
    "6#ד": "For behold it was nullified in water; even according to Ran in Nedarim that heter in heter is not nullified — nevertheless it does not become nevelah; and if so when it falls from the milk only according to value and there is sixty and it is permitted:",
    "6#ב": "He increases upon it and nullifies it; and in Orach Chayyim regarding permitted oil he ruled one cannot nullify it — see Peleti that there is distinction between wet and dry and between muktzeh issur which is deoraita-like and other issurim:",
    "6#ג": "Returns and stirs — Pri Chadash expanded on this root; and see Peleti in all poskim's approaches; for practical halachah one should not be lenient against Rama — see there:"
  },
  "mateh-yehonatan": {
    "1#_": "(Siman 99 in Shulchan Aruch seif 2) In what case is this stated that issur bones combine with heter, etc.; but if it was cooked first and afterward fell, etc. [The taste in this is explained in Rosh chapter 5 of Terumot and Rosh chapter 5 of Hullin and Tur because we are concerned lest the issur diminished and was absorbed in the bones; and accordingly if we saw the issur while it was raw, certainly sixty suffices against the meat seen while the piece was still raw; and so Bach explicitly; and it appears that even according to Rama who holds we say chein nafsho in other issurim and even in a matter that has no taste by itself we say chein nafsho — nevertheless here he would agree, as Shach wrote s.k. 3, that a bone is like a metal vessel; and if so, likewise if it was cooked first with the bones too the halachah is thus that bones combine with heter if they knew how much meat measure there was at first. End of the words of Rav HaGadol R' Shlomo Cohen Matz, first chapter Vilna]:",
    "2#_": "(There in the Hagahah) And some are stringent, etc.; and in a place of loss, etc., one may rely on those who are lenient, for such is essential; and see Shach s.k. 3 what he challenges on Bach; and according to what I wrote, there is emission for bones by themselves — a question on Bach that he did not say sixty is required against the bone itself, for certainly the bone itself does not become nevelah, only since it has its own emission the emission becomes nevelah. And it appears to resolve Shach's words — nevertheless he challenges Bach well: behold that which we say chein nafsho in other issurim is because it applies to basar b'chalav, that is in the substance of the food; but in emission there is no basar upon it — it is like water mere, and it itself cannot come to eating until it is cooked with something else and expels its absorption to the dish; and since there is no reality for this emission to stand by itself, it is not comparable to basar b'chalav and we do not say chein nafsho — investigate:",
    "3#_": "(There seif 5) One does not nullify issur, etc.; if b'shogeg permitted, and if b'mezid forbidden. And Pri Chadash challenged — this is against the Gemara in Gittin daf 53 and 54 that challenges R' Yosi against R' Yosi regarding cooking on Shabbat b'shogeg — if so we penalize shogeg like mezid; and the Mishnah \"one who medameh and one who menasech\" — R' Yosi says b'shogeg exempt, b'mezid liable, and we do not penalize; and it answers this is d'oraisa and this is d'rabbanan, etc.; if so here where there is issur d'oraisa, why do we not penalize shogeg like mezid? And it appears to me to resolve — this case is different: there regarding cooking on Shabbat, even b'shogeg the Shabbat desecration — the essence of the issur is the cooking that he did melachah on Shabbat; and so in metamei, medameh, menasech — if it were d'oraisa we would penalize shogeg like mezid, because to tamei terumah or medameh in non-sacred produce is itself nullifying issur even if he did not intend for this; unlike here regarding nullifying issur there is no issur at all in intending to nullify; but if he nullifies b'shogeg and did not intend to nullify at all, only that it fell by itself — there is no issur at all here and it is not relevant to penalize shogeg like mezid; and see Taz seif 8 what he challenges in Tur's words against each other — in siman 100 specifically if it fell, but if he threw it, even shogeg we penalize, etc.; and here he wrote we do not penalize; and I would say there is a great distinction: there the beginning of the decree was if he threw it does not help, for otherwise what did the Sages accomplish in their enactment; they enacted an important matter of 32 — if he threw it helps, how can one say an important matter of 32? Or the Sages' enactment was even if he threw; and if so since the essence of the decree was from the start not to throw b'mezid, well we decree shogeg like mezid; unlike here in nullifying issur where the entire essence of the decree was not to nullify issur l'chatchila, but the prohibition to eat is only because of penalty in such a case — we do not penalize shogeg like mezid; we say we decree shogeg, etc., but penalizing shogeg like mezid we do not say; and it may be resolved: there well we penalize shogeg like mezid because the entire reason is in falling one of them to the sea that is permitted because we say the heter fell on the surface that it should have fallen from the majority — behold the heter is thousands; nevertheless they say issur fell to the sea and the heter remained; and all this in falling by itself; but if he threw it we penalize shogeg — they said and they said lest issur fell; but in nullifying issur the reasoning permits, for behold the issur is not sensed, only they penalized him because they enacted not to nullify issur l'chatchila on the surface that it is permitted now since behold the issur is not sensed; and all this b'mezid; but shogeg — some say we do not penalize, for behold the taste of issur is not sensed at all — investigate. And what Taz wrote — if it is issur nullified in the majority, even according to the one who penalizes shogeg like mezid, in such a case all agree we do not penalize — it appears to me it is proven not better than shogeg; he wrote the teacher need not investigate if it increased afterward — if it is not known that there is issur in it, permitted, for we establish all Torah prohibitions — if he increased upon them b'shogeg, permitted, for we do not penalize; if so it appears that according to the one who penalizes it is forbidden — or that it is not better than shogeg; and that which Tosafot wrote see there — it appears he was explaining their own words in what they wrote that there is according to the one who says b'shogeg permitted; and lest he is shogeg, etc., is another explanation; and that is even according to the one who penalizes, saying permitted is better; but the truth is not so; Tosafot wanted to resolve their own words in what they wrote that there is according to the one who says b'shogeg permitted and they challenged what they themselves wrote in abtlinhu — it appears b'mezid; and on this he wrote that even when nullifying b'mezid, since he thinks it permitted to nullify, he is like shogeg; and there is no place for Taz's words at all — investigate:",
    "4#_": "(There in the Hagahah) Investigate — dry mixed with dry, or even wet in wet. And that which Rama wrote stam and did not distinguish between basar b'chalav and other issurim — in basar b'chalav certainly we do not say chein nafsho wet in wet, since certainly a drop of milk that mixed has sixty against the meat is milk forbidden in its own right; if so the mixture should be forbidden because it can be squeezed; and some say it deals with where there is sixty against the entire mixture — for this he disagreed between wet in wet and dry in dry; except regarding that piece we need not chein nafsho; and as Shach wrote s.k. 10 — and that Shach wrote in basar b'chalav one need not investigate — that is the reason in wet in wet we do not say chein nafsho, see there; still it is difficult — if before us a piece that a drop of milk fell on it that fell on the piece, why did Tur not write that in such a case one must investigate? And one must say this need not be written, for certainly otherwise one must investigate lest it was placed entirely outside the sauce — if so sixty is required against the entire piece; if so certainly one comes to investigate the entire action and Tur need not write a general rule; and what Shach challenges on Darkhei Moshe from the drop of milk nevelah, etc. — see siman 93 and you will find proper resolution:",
    "5#_": "(There seif 6) A d'rabbanan issur, etc.; and see Shach what he challenges from permitted Chanukah oil; and it appears I would say to resolve — behold Tosafot in Beitzah there challenge from igulei develah — why not add to it and nullify; and explain this case is different — there the essence is d'oraisa, and Shulchan Aruch did not disagree so; and it is difficult on him Tosafot's difficulty from terumah; and one may say — behold Tosafot chapter 39 wrote in the name of Hagahot Asheri: if d'rabbanan issur mixed one-in-one, permitted; two-in-noten ta'am — however Rosh in the first chapter of Beitzah disagrees on this; and if so one may say — behold for brevity one may explain Tosafot's difficulty another way — even though one-in-one is forbidden also in d'rabbanan issur and we do not say safek lekula since issur was established, nevertheless it is still called sixty at least; and according to this there is a question: granted regarding branches, since already mixed one-in-one there is no doubt upon it and in sixty d'rabbanan they say one increases and nullifies; but certainly d'rabbanan cannot increase and nullify; accordingly in terumah they needed one hundred one to nullify it; if so without less than this it is not sixty d'rabbanan but certainly; and certainly it does not help [however from what Maharam of Rottenburg must rule — that is because he does not want to disagree between essence d'oraisa and no essence d'oraisa; therefore Tosafot's difficulty from terumah is difficult for him; or that one may not increase where there is issur and it is not light issur; if so until here one must say one-in-one is not called sixty d'rabbanan; otherwise one need not rule like Maharam — Tosafot's difficulty otherwise has a question; or that he does not hold like Or Zarua but that it is not sixty d'rabbanan]; and all this in dry in dry where there is no rabbinic enactment in its nullification; but wet in wet requires sixty even d'rabbanan in mashahu — if so certainly wherever there is not sixty here it is not sixty at all, as in terumah I mentioned; and forbidden to increase upon it; accordingly Shach's difficulty from Chanukah lamp — there it is wet in wet like certainly d'rabbanan issur and forbidden to increase upon it. And it may be resolved — it is in the first chapter of Beitzah daf 2 side 2 \"an egg born on Shabbat and an egg born on Yom Tov — one may not move,\" etc.; and safek forbidden — granted according to Ravah who says because preparation — safek d'oraisa for stringency; but according to Rav Yosef and R' Yitzchak it is safek d'rabbanan for leniency, etc.; and Tosafot wrote but according to Rabbenu who says because muktzeh does not apply except muktzeh is strict and is like d'oraisa; and one may say what Tosafot wrote regarding branches that fell from the palm — muktzeh that has no essence from d'oraisa is lighter than other d'rabbanan issurim that have essence from the Torah — contradicts what he wrote here that muktzeh is strict over other issurim; and one may say Tosafot wrote it is strict for R' Yehudah who has muktzeh certainly strict over other issurim; but for us who hold like R' Shimon who has no muktzeh except on Yom Tov we are stringent like R' Yehudah — certainly muktzeh is not so strict and is lighter than other d'rabbanan issurim that have essence from d'oraisa; and all this regarding muktzeh because of issur or muktzeh m'da'at; but muktzeh because of mitzvah that even R' Shimon has this muktzeh which is strict and is like d'oraisa — accordingly Shulchan Aruch well regarding permitted oil in Chanukah lamp which is muktzeh because of mitzvah which is like d'oraisa, forbidden to add to it; and there is no question:",
    "6#_": "(There in the Hagahah) Issur that was nullified, etc. — returns and stirs, etc. Ran challenged — if so if a kezayit of tallow or nevelah fell into the dish, why does sixty help to nullify it? Let us say it absorbs and returns and expels until there is not sixty here; and from this difficulty he derived we do not say returns-and-stirs without knowledge; if so Rambam in Forbidden Foods even without knowledge we say returns-and-stirs — Ran's difficulty stands; and one must say as Peirush HaMishnayot wrote — there is only one absorption and one expulsion; and see Shach s.k. 21 who wrote in dry in dry we do not say returns-and-stirs and they challenge him from Mishnah se'ah of terumah that fell to a hundred of non-sacred and he did not manage to raise it and another fell — forbidden; and R' Shimon permits — how much may he increase until permitted until the majority measure; it appears specifically until like the majority; but equal-equal we say returns-and-stirs; and behold for brevity even for Ran there are challenges from the Mishnah he wrote that we do not say returns-and-stirs — for otherwise if forbidden fell to heter let us say it absorbs and expels and returns and absorbs and expels so that there would not be majority heter; one may say that which explains until how much he may increase until the majority measure — that is only d'rabbanan; but d'oraisa even less than majority we do not say returns-and-stirs; if so here with kezayit nevelah that fell to heter, since it cannot be otherwise, the Sages placed on d'oraisa that we do not say returns-and-stirs even in less than majority heter; but Shach who ruled so for us is difficult; and one may say by force — behold the poskim disagree in this that we hold dry in dry one-in-two nullified — there are poskim since there is one heter more than issur, even if a hundred of issur fell to a hundred and one of heter, even so they say nullified since there is one more than issur; and some say on the view that there are two times heter, such as two of issur one must say four of heter; if so one may say Shach holds certainly even in dry in dry we say returns-and-stirs if issur and heter are equal without majority heter, even one-and-one; and that which he wrote we do not say returns-and-stirs — that is he holds two times heter is required; and on this he wrote if it mixed so there is no majority against issur — majority that is two times heter — nevertheless we do not say returns-and-stirs; but nevertheless there must be majority against issur, only he need not have two times against the issur; if so that which the Mishnah teaches until how much he may increase — that is that there must be at least majority heter and not equal — investigate:",
    "7#_": "(There seif 7) If a small amount of issur was absorbed, etc. — permitted to use, etc. And behold the Ra'ah in Bedek HaBayit — his view is that even in such a case it is forbidden to use l'chatchila; however there is proof like Rashba — behold ta'aruvat kelim we derive from gentile utensils; and behold according to Peirush HaMishnayot we hold that even according to the one who says ta'aruvat kelim is not d'oraisa, nevertheless if issur mixed in less than majority heter, certainly ta'aruvat kelim is d'oraisa; if so it is difficult how we derive from gentile utensils ta'aruvat kelim — perhaps forever ta'aruvat kelim in the majority some say; and that which they required purging vessels from the law — that is from concern lest he use a small amount in a way that there would not be majority heter against issur; or that where one's way is to use abundantly we are not concerned lest he use the minority — investigate:"
  },
  "nekudot-hakesef": {
    "1#_": "(Siman 99 in Taz s.k. 8) And according to this there is a question, etc. In vain he expanded on this, for Beit Yosef also concludes there is a distinction between nullifying issur and throwing one to the sea:",
    "2#_": "(There) It is very astonishing, for we also require sixty against the pot itself. There is a question — for Beit Yosef wrote above siman 93 that it is praiseworthy that the pot's width be large 22 that it holds water so there is sixty against the entire pot, such as when its copper is thin and the width large, end quote; if so it deals here that the pot is not full, only cooked in part of it. Also what Beit Yosef wrote there regarding known measure of meat cooked in it — deals with Ittur's view, etc. — also here there is a question; one may also say here that it was known before they put the food in the pot how much its measure was, and therefore certainly one need not now estimate more than what was put in the pot — investigate:",
    "3#_": "(There s.k. 8) And therefore Tur learned from Semag's view, etc. Regarding stirring the pot, etc. — all this is forced; and also in siman 92 itself we do not say stirring the pot helps afterward; rather its explanation there is a different matter, as Shach wrote there s.k. 8; but in Sifrei I wrote another resolution that is correct and true — from the language of Shulchan Aruch Mechaber it appears that the water added afterward does not combine except because all issurim that one increased upon them do not help, for we penalize shogeg like mezid; and that which we need throughout the Talmud the reason of chein nafsho — that is where even shogeg does not exist, such as it fell by itself as R' Yosef explained (if so it deals with dry in dry; but in wet in wet Sefer HaMitzvot holds we do not say \"they increased b'shogeg permitted\" as Beit Yosef brought this distinction in the name of Hagahot ShaDa); and now for Shulchan Aruch Mechaber even where chein nafsho does not apply one must ask, such as where it was forbidden because of mashahu and the like, as above siman 92 in Tur and Ran; and therefore Tur challenged him and it appears one need not ask from Taz since we hold all issurim that one increased upon them b'shogeg are permitted — investigate:",
    "5#_": "(Hagahah in Shach s.k. 15) And if it were so it should have been resolved that there it deals with without knowledge, etc. And it appears to me to distinguish — granted this law he wrote the reason is we consider as if the issur fell now or a piece of meat fell into milk and now there is sixty in sixty without knowledge; but in milk the taste of milk was absorbed in meat and if so the milk is as if it is a piece of meat whose taste is in milk and milk in milk is min b'mino — therefore it is not nullified — investigate. Also what he wrote \"and that which we learned in chapter 5 of Terumot,\" etc. — also here it appears to explain that which we learned in chapter 5 of Terumot — this is what it says: if b'shogeg, meaning without knowledge of the mixture, and it mixed b'shogeg permitted; and if b'mezid, even if the mixture is not known, only that it mixed b'mezid for another reason, nevertheless forbidden, for we decree b'mezid lest he mix even when known; but b'shogeg we do not decree shogeg against known, since b'shogeg with knowledge is only a general stringency and we do not decree; and with this the Gemara's difficulty on Rama is resolved. End of hagahah:",
    "6#_": "(Hagahah in Shach s.k. 19) And forced; and further this \"and some say,\" etc. And it appears to me since muktzeh branches are d'oraisa as it is (in Eruvin) [in Pesachim] daf 47b (see there the conclusion is not so) — strict like muktzeh for its mitzvah; and therefore he was forced to resolve another explanation — investigate:"
  },
  "peleti": {
    "1#א": "Issur bones, etc. — Ri'az in the name of a Gaon brought that this applies in its species — d'oraisa in the majority is nullified, but in not its species not; and the Rav author of Pri Chadash held to his view since they have no expulsion from themselves — like a pot, for we say \"is heter absorbed and issur not?\" — why should it combine to nullify, and why should the pottery body not combine? Therefore this is only in its species which is only shiyur d'rabbanan; and the Rav in several places wrote it does not appear so to Rashba and Rosh; and the correct view is that besides since they wrote stam and did not distinguish, they also challenged in what the Gemara says according to the one who says in sixty \"meat with bones we estimate\" — it appears bones do not combine to nullify issur; and according to Ri'az's view this is only asmakhta they learned from not its species, for in its species in the majority it is nullified and they only said in sixty because it applies to not its species; and in not its species indeed bones do not combine to nullify issur; and conversely according to Pri Chadash's words it appears even heter bones in not its species should not be combined, for it is like a pot; therefore it appears they wondered how the Gemara says according to the one who says in sixty \"meat with bones we estimate\" — behold bones combine to nullify issur; and see Rosh who forced that in the Gemara it is only asmakhta mere; and already the Ra'ah wondered in Beit HaYayin; therefore what appears in Pri Chadash's difficulties it appears Rosh etc. answered and said one may say there is moisture in bones, nevertheless it is not better than gid moisture, for we hold there is not noten ta'am in gid; therefore in truth there is weak taste in them, for the palate will testify if one cooks bones in water they change the water in taste and color, only as we must say in this that they disagree whether there is noten ta'am in gid or not — certainly they do not disagree about the reality of the palate tasting, as Rashba wrote — only weak taste exists and they disagree whether it is full taste; and if so even if we disagree whether there is noten ta'am in gid, certainly weak taste exists in bones like gid and not full taste like what the Torah forbade; but in any event since there is taste, even if extremely weak, at least it helps nullify issur taste like water and is not comparable to a pot that has no expulsion at all. And if so Pri Chadash's difficulty is resolved correctly; but accordingly according to the one who says there is noten ta'am in gid who holds even weak taste the Torah forbade — even in bones one may say so to be strict in the difficulty; one must understand that the Gemara seeks support from foreleg cooking to shiyur issurim — even Rashi wrote explicitly the conclusion is we estimate in meat all around, and this is in sixty as known, as the Gemara says; and one must say those amora'im of tradition hold there is gid as Rashi wrote and as appears from the Gemara; and this case is different — gid whose taste is weak for all, and if so they needed to doubt shiyur noten; but according to the one who says there is not noten ta'am in gid and they do not disagree about the conclusion that in this we estimate in meat all around — the dispute is difficult; and as Rambam wrote; and end here it was other issurim; and accordingly this foreleg cooking sugya is all according to the one who says there is noten ta'am in gid; and if so well according to the one who says in sixty that bones have weak expulsion and Pri Chadash's difficulty that it is no better than gid — there is a question, for in truth issur bones forbid and heter bones nullify; and the other view holds like the poskim that they have no expulsion at all and are not comparable to gid, therefore they are not included in issur and also not in gravy, as Pri Chadash wrote they have no expulsion at all. But still difficult is Yerushalmi that asked if heter bones combine to nullify and concludes heter shells raise the forbidden — how do we learn one from its counterpart regarding bones even in not its species, and shells one must say in dry and its species; and how do we learn one from its counterpart? But in truth in Yerushalmi chapter 3 it asked in this whether in a hundred or sixty and says according to the one who says in a hundred you remove issur bones — for that view one must say they have no expulsion as written above; and if so well it asked afterward even heter bones do not help, for it is like a pot; and it answers you cannot, for the Sages made it like its species so heter shells help to nullify; and even here in its species it is only stringency mere to help nullify and is only asmakhta that is only in its species. Yerushalmi's words are correct; but according to the one who says in sixty he holds bones have weak expulsion and therefore they forbid and heter ones help — investigate. But this is according to the one who says there is noten ta'am in gid — if so this weak expulsion for issur is no better than gid and permitted helps nullify — there is a question; and the Gemara's words are correct and the law is true law: and that which Yerushalmi says terumah in onion in egg and the vegetable and the water and shells combine — even though shells have no expulsion and are no better than a bowl and it is not its species — there is a question; one may also say it deals where there are also eggs; and d'oraisa in the majority is nullified; and even though vegetable and water are not its species, etc.; and this is proof according to Rashba in siman the blood that even on not its species they say etc. and egg without noten ta'am so much that they considered estimating in mashahu and fifty as explained in the Gemara and Yerushalmi only stringently in sixty because not to distinguish among other issurim; and in any event shells combine lest all shells exceed this measure, as Shach wrote explicitly in siman chapter 6 s.k. 15 — see there — investigate between in its species and not its species; and there is a question; and all Pri Chadash's and Rosh's difficulties are resolved:",
    "4#_": "\"This applies in not its species, etc.\" — this is Rashba's view and the reason: it appears it deals where we knew how much issur there was when it fell and we estimate against all of it; otherwise even in its species one cannot estimate — are you saying heter absorbed and issur not absorbed? — why not in not its species; and one must say as if the pot absorbed from water to water why not enter that absorption into the calculation, for it also absorbs and expels; and one must say we are not clear what the pot absorbed in cooking whether it fell or receives from issur; and if so even in this lest a spoonful of known mixture gave issur taste to heter already absorbed in the pot and this does not enter the calculation as if absorbed from water — what Rashba wrote and afterward the issur in heter taste and there is not sixty here; therefore heter itself needs sixty and that is what appears Rashba's reason for stringency: and behold Tosafot separated in what we say regarding the pot itself — the explanation is not against the pot's earthenware, only if it absorbed two zaytim from water and afterward issur zayit — nevertheless if it expels also the two zaytim absorbed from water they combine to nullify; and if it does not expel, also issur does not expel; and it appears Tosafot hold we do not say in absorbed piece it becomes nevelah — for otherwise those two zaytim already became nevelah and forced to say it deals with absorbing meat, for still heter — this was not mentioned in Tosafot; and accordingly one may say the latter version that speaks in what the pot absorbed does not disagree on this halachah, only holds the explanation of pot is a different matter; but for halachah he agrees to this law; and from this Issur Veheter end general principle derives so too if two handfuls stuck absorbed — the second absorbed combines to nullify the first absorbed; and that is as appears if necessarily if it expels this too expels and if not this too does not expel — and these are Tosafot's words as they are; and in necessarily in pot or two handfuls when they are one species; therefore he wrote if the pot is metal and spoon metal this necessarily does not apply since one cannot expel more than its fellow — but these pot and spoon are also metal, both can necessarily expel, only afterward scrubbing to be strict in pot perhaps spoon touches spoon in pot and pot absorbs from it and becomes nevelah the absorbed and afterward expels; and if so how can absorbed in pot that is already chein nafsho combine — it is stringency mere; and in any event its law is founded on Tosafot's words as solid proof; and from kli sheni in its species that even if we say the latter version disagrees in a d'rabbanan matter we act leniently as Rosh and Beit Yosef explicitly; and if so clear law from Tosafot's view. And I do not know what the Rav author of Lechem Mishneh and Rav Peirush HaMishnayot end seif 8 wondered on Issur Veheter as if he invented law from his heart — behold they are Tosafot's words and a great man spoke — one does not reject him; and truth is other authors did not explain like Tosafot's explanation; but for that one need not dismiss their words, especially since there is reasoning; and in particular it is more reasonable to estimate in pot earthenware and plain:",
    "1#ב": "And in a place of loss, etc. Bach challenged — behold it is issur davuk; and Tosafot Shach — a bone is like a metal vessel since hagalah helps and not chein nafsho; and according to what I wrote that there is emission — one may question, for this emission what of it that it is weak, nevertheless it becomes nevelah; but otherwise difficult — behold all taste for issur issur davuk lest in pouring it remains below in a way that there is not sixty; and accordingly Bach is challenged well from this reason we forbid davuk; therefore in truth one must say it deals where the bone is not stuck only placed in the pot:",
    "5#א": "Issur, etc.; and Raavad's view is it is d'oraisa; and one must understand — since this foreleg of ram gives taste in ram it is issur d'oraisa and it is a novelty that the Torah equated foreleg cooking — if so how did they permit cooking in a pot of sin-offering peace offerings; and what he wrote foreleg with ram and sin-offering and peace offerings and noten ta'am bar noten ta'am there is not — behold sin-offering issur for non-priests like foreleg ram for ram; and why would it be called issur and sin-offering to peace offerings would be in the category of heter; and what Tosafot and Rosh wrote consecrated vessels we hold d'oraisa does not apply — for forbidden to nullify d'oraisa; and one must say absorbed in vessel is not considered nullifying issur until they said chein nafsho name does not apply; and also there full issur is not called upon it and is not in the category of nullifying issur; if so foreleg in ram is well in the category of nullifying issur:",
    "5#ב": "If b'shogeg permitted — Pri Chadash expanded to be strict in not its species, for we hold in Torah we penalize shogeg like mezid; according to the one who says one may not nullify issur is only d'rabbanan — words are not understood at all; for if they permit b'shogeg he will do b'mezid and transgress Torah issur like cooking on Shabbat and planting in sheviit; but here what concern if he nullifies b'shogeg he will nullify b'mezid — nevertheless there is no Torah issur, for from Torah permitted to nullify; and what concern that the issur itself of Torah — and truth is Gemara's language forced that it says \"and so regarding split nuts\" — from Torah one-in-two nullified and R' Yosi acquired; it appears where from Torah not nullified only in sixty — well R' Yosi penalized; but in truth the Gemara challenges so — let us say forbidden to nullify issur from Torah; and if so one may say lest they come to nullify; and for this the Gemara says in the Gemara: granted if not yet nullified; but this that was already nullified from Torah stands one-in-two nullified — if so no longer applicable to nullify d'oraisa for behold already nullified:",
    "5#ג": "Permitted, etc.; Taz s.k. 8 challenged Tur's words against each other — here ruled we penalize shogeg like mezid and in siman 110 ruled like R' Yosi who penalizes; and explain to distinguish — all penalty lest he be b'mezid and say he was shogeg; if so where he has migo of silence this does not apply; but in siman 110 deals where the act is known to the public and if so he cannot hide the matter and has no migo; therefore we penalize; and his words are unclear — in falling to the sea we penalize shogeg stam teaches even for himself and not known at all to others nevertheless we penalize even though he could hide the entire matter; in truth one may explain well all Semag's concern requiring investigation is lest he did not know there is issur in the matter and he added to the pot because afterward he will be careful and also say to the teacher such happened to me; and if so shogeg in this all agree we do not penalize as Rosh wrote below siman 110 if he threw to the sea known mixture permitted to everyone; and see Issur Veheter general principle so too; and it appears also ben yomo there issur is not nullified only permitted through end of seif; but issur exists until one forbidden to eat them; and if so b'mezid he does end of seif by hand — we do not say; and therefore they decreed shogeg like mezid; but here when the forbidding factor was nullified and issur returned to heter we do not say against mezid, for even b'mezid there is no issur here; and therefore ben yomo wrote there is disagreement between this here and that of falling; and in truth were it not for concern I would say Tosafot's intent for halachah is not to decree falling shogeg like mezid — Tosafot separated so in Zevachim for Rav's matter who said fell to the sea and separated fell but not threw — that is according to Rav who holds in Hullin daf 15 Rav like R' Meir; and for R' Meir we say d'oraisa we do not penalize but d'rabbanan we penalize shogeg like mezid; but for us who hold like R' Yosi it is reversed; but my mind turned against Tur and Rosh; and in any event there is disagreement as he wrote; and Taz wrote from Tosafot Bekhorot daf 23 it appears saying permitted all hold we do not penalize — and it is not so, for Tosafot challenged there in the Gemara savar abtlinhu in the majority — what will it help whether shogeg or mezid; however there is according to the one who says there b'shogeg permitted; and lest this is considered shogeg thinking permitted to nullify — end of his words; and Taz understood this lest is a separate explanation and thinks permitted to nullify for all permitted; and this is not so — this refutes that explanation that there is according to the one who says shogeg permitted; and if this is not called shogeg for behold he intends to nullify — for this Tosafot wrote also this that since he thinks permitted to do it, it can be called shogeg; and this is clear in Tosafot's intent; and so Magen Avraham Orach Chayyim beginning siman 318 that even one who acted on rabbi's ruling that erred is called shogeg — see there:",
    "5#ד": "And forbidden to sell it also, etc.; Shach's view in permitted benefit — if he does not sell to an Israelite at a higher price than he sold to a non-Jew, permitted; and Pri Chadash's view even in such a case permitted, for we hold \"one who gives to a non-Jew to castrate his animal\" — permitted to sell to a non-Jew even though he sold through castration at a high price, only that he is forced to sell; and his words are unclear — for there they did not castrate, he could have kept for himself; and now that they castrated he is forced to sell — obviously it is penalty; but here where issur mixed in heter he also was forced to sell to a non-Jew, for to Israel forbidden; and now that it was nullified if you permit him to sell to Israel at high price behold there is no penalty at all and he is not penalized at all; on the contrary he benefits and profits — what penalty is here; therefore correct like Shach's words:",
    "5#ה": "Or even wet in wet, etc.; so Derishah uPerishah resolved Tur who wrote stam the teacher need not investigate when water was added even though it is hora'ah in basar b'chalav that for all chein nafsho; and therefore wet in wet we do not say chein nafsho; and Shach and Pri Chadash challenged that in basar b'chalav certainly we say chein nafsho; and proof from that milk nevelah; and I already expanded above siman 92 in resolving the matter; however I did not understand with all this Rama's explanation of a drop of milk that fell to pot with meat and sauce — if there was not sixty among all meat forbidden; and if afterward they added water — truth sauce is not chein nafsho for wet in wet; nevertheless meat is chein nafsho; and if the teacher did not know and estimates as it is now even meat permitted. Also Rashba's words who disagrees between can-be-squeezed wet in wet — that appears in cold like two hundred fell to a pit and the like; but issur that was cooked with a wet thing behold the wet thing absorbed in hot and will not expel again — what will help if it mixed afterward; and see below siman 106 what he wrote to resolve; and in any event this is not explanation for Tur — rather Tur agrees in basar b'chalav only challenges Semak who ruled so in other issurim too:",
    "5#ו": "And some say, etc.; Shach s.k. 15 challenged Issur Veheter in what he wrote even where chein nafsho if not known in between and heter increased nevertheless permitted — for if so what does the Gemara challenge milk nevelah — that perhaps deals with not known in between; and what he challenged from medameh and vinegar — for the one who says chein nafsho in all issurim one may say not known in between; and so several more difficulties; and behold Issur Veheter did not answer from his heart but his words need to be said in Tosafot Bekhorot 22 s.v. yativ, etc. — who wrote if there is not sixty of heter relative to issur and two hundred for soaking — if he adds heter forever forbidden and other issurim permitted that he increases so much there is sixty against heter; and lest even in nullified we do not say first-first nullified like meat piece that absorbed nevelah taste even according to the one who says chein nafsho in other issurim like basar b'chalav who do not say piece becomes nevelah except that increasing does not help emission because it cannot be squeezed; but increasing helps — end of his words; behold he wrote explicitly increasing helps; and it appears an astonishing matter — in the Gemara we say why is milk permitted, etc., for increasing does not help in several places; and what forced according to R' Tam without saying R' Eliezer's explanation from Tosafot's difficulty that even according to R' Eliezer still need sixty only against nevelah; and for explanation it appears also for R' Yosi difficult — behold increasing helps afterward; and how need shiyur against entire piece; and therefore he holds. To Issur Veheter the long version — all this without knowledge in between; but with knowledge chein nafsho entirely — investigate; and it appears when we disagree between known and unknown — that is in other issurim which is d'rabbanan and they said to disagree between known and unknown; known mixture is not the issur but in basar b'chalav applies even other issurim for the one who says chein nafsho d'oraisa certainly no distinction between known and unknown — all sugyot there in Hullin for the one who says chein nafsho in other issurim d'oraisa as written above siman 92 at length — investigate: and what Issur Veheter the long version wrote in nullifying issur to disagree between known and unknown — Shach challenged how do we say b'mezid forbidden — how is without knowledge mezid relevant; and one may reject — plainly the Gemara matter of increasing b'shogeg permitted and b'mezid forbidden all deals with after it became known; only Issur Veheter rules like R' Yosi even b'shogeg forbidden; and on this Issur Veheter descended to disagree; and the practical difference after it became known; but known mixture for all permitted and Issur Veheter rules so; and if so all Shach's difficulties in the Gemara as Shach wrote are only for halachah Issur Veheter holds as he wrote — investigate:",
    "6#א": "A d'rabbanan issur, etc. — one increases and nullifies; and the later ones challenged from Orach Chayyim siman 677 regarding Chanukah lamp oil that mixed with other oil without sixty that one may not add to nullify — on the surface d'rabbanan; and forced to distinguish — Chanukah since designated for its mitzvah is stricter; Tur there brought the law in the name of Maharam of Rottenburg and wrote not comparable to branches that fell from the palm that one increases and nullifies — this case is different there the issur is light and he does not benefit from them until removal time — end here; and I expanded in my novellae on Yom Tov laws; and in brief it is thus: in dry in dry each piece is not certainly issur only safek issur; therefore see below siman 109 what he wrote in the name of Hagahot Rosh in one-in-one in d'rabbanan issur permitted for two noten ta'am — each one is safek and safek d'rabbanan lekula; and if so Tur's view permits to nullify it — even though we say d'rabbanan issur may not be nullified — the practical difference is certainly issur; but sixty certainly issur has leniency to nullify; therefore branches that fell from the palm one increases and nullifies — it is only safek; but wet in wet mixes without sixty and if so behold it is mixed certainly issur and is mixed certainly issur not safek issur — for each drop has issur; Tur holds to be strict even in d'rabbanan forbidden to nullify since it is certainly issur; therefore in Chanukah lamp oil which is wet in wet forbidden to nullify; and no need for Maharam's taste reason that it is light; and also the difficulty on Beit Yosef from Tosafot 15 that permits cooking gentile butter without intent to nullify scraped milk fat that is d'rabbanan issur without essence from Torah — without permitted to nullify; and it resolves that in wet in wet even d'rabbanan may not nullify: and further another reason — in the Gemara challenges on that of branches that fell, etc.; such a case according to Rav Ashi who says a matter that has permitted use even in d'rabbanan safek forbidden — how does increasing branches help? Explanation one must say the Gemara holds if in d'rabbanan safek forbidden if so made like deoraita if so also nullifying issur forbidden like deoraita; and difficult why specifically according to Rav Ashi — the difficulty for all; Tosafot challenged there sleeping — the Gemara challenges for the one who says because liquids flowed because fallen fruits in Beitzah — why safek forbidden; it is safek d'rabbanan; and they resolved muktzeh is like deoraita; and one may say what Tosafot wrote regarding branches that fell from the palm — muktzeh that has no essence from d'oraisa is lighter than other d'rabbanan issurim that have essence from Torah — contradicts what he wrote here muktzeh is strict over other issurim; and one may say Tosafot wrote it is strict for R' Yehudah; but for us who hold like R' Shimon only on Yom Tov stringent like R' Yehudah — certainly muktzeh is not so strict and is lighter than other d'rabbanan issurim with essence from d'oraisa; and all this regarding muktzeh because of issur or muktzeh m'da'at; but muktzeh because of mitzvah that even R' Shimon has — is strict and like d'oraisa — accordingly Shulchan Aruch well regarding permitted Chanukah oil which is muktzeh because of mitzvah forbidden to add; and no question:",
    "6#ב": "D'rabbanan issur. I already wrote resolution of what was difficult from Chanukah lamp; and I wrote in novellae on Rambam on Yom Tov laws — seek and find at length:",
    "6#ג": "Returns and stirs. Pri Chadash expanded greatly on this root and stood on investigation to clarify; and I will bring in his words briefly to clarify the law on clear foundations with Heaven's help. And behold first Rambam ruled in Laws of Terumah — se'ah of terumah that fell to a hundred of non-sacred and he did not manage to raise it until another fell — with knowledge it is not forbidden; and if not, forbidden. And from this Pri Chadash ruled Rambam's view in all issurim that after it became known we do not say returns-and-stirs and do not challenge on ben yomo who wrote there is no learning from terumah — Rambam said only in terumah; but in other issurim knowledge does not depend at all; and Pri Chadash challenged — behold in siman 111 we learn many laws from terumah; and what he wrote terumah and what he wrote other issurim regarding knowledge; also he expanded why Rambam ruled like R' Shimon that knowledge permits and not like the Sages that raising permits. And I wonder how Rambam can hold in all issurim that known mixture — we say returns-and-stirs — behold Rambam in Laws of Forbidden Foods chapter 16 wrote explicitly: yayin nesech that fell to a cistern and not its species — if it is a small jar, if he pours every day, nevertheless it does not return and stir; and he does not distinguish at all between known or after knowledge. Rather Rambam's view appears — specifically in terumah we say returns-and-stirs since raising is required; and so too that he did not raise there forbids upon it; therefore we say returns-and-stirs; if so in other issurim raising is not required — we do not say returns-and-stirs; and R' Shimon for his reason holds all that stands to be raised is like moram; therefore knowledge is required for R' Shimon — for when we say stands to be raised that is when he knows from it then it stands to be raised; but when he has no knowledge it does not stand to be raised for he did not know at all, etc.; for this we do not say it is like moram; therefore R' Shimon requires knowledge and the Sages disagree — R' Shimon holds we do not say all that stands to be raised is like moram as Rosh wrote; therefore raising is required; and if so we hold like R' Shimon in this as Rambam wrote once in Laws of Food Impurity — a cow renders food impurity for all that stands to be redeemed is like redeemed as explained in the Gemara; if so here too halachah is like R' Shimon; therefore Rambam ruled knowledge prevents returns-and-stirs — then we say like moram; and that is in terumah; but in issurim raising is never required — it does not return and-stir since raising is not needed — what need for knowledge; and Rambam's words are clear and correct: and behold Raavad also holds we do not say in other issurim returns-and-stirs only in terumah since raising is required; therefore in terumah he holds like the Sages knowledge also does not help — the taste since raising is required; and Pri Chadash challenged him since in dry in dry if it increased so much until majority issur — for all we say returns-and-stirs as explained in the Mishnah; and challenged what he wrote majority regarding dry and what he wrote noten ta'am regarding not its species (and this challenge does not apply to Rambam who holds ta'aruvat kelim some say; and if so majority d'oraisa and noten ta'am d'rabbanan — only for Raavad who holds d'oraisa it is difficult): however certainly if this were until majority it would be a good claim; but clear that d'oraisa even majority — since already nullified issur returns to be heter only the Sages were strict to forbid when the majority is issur; and if so one cannot compare and challenge rabbinic decrees against each other — in one they decreed there is majority issur and in noten ta'am they did not decree; and proof it is only a rabbinic single decree — if so why specifically terumah increased; even half-and-half also d'oraisa issur as explained below siman 109 and 110 piece milk and piece fat — one who eats from it liable asham talui; and we say in chapter Ha'Orlah in Torah issur on the surface that they did not increase half-on-half like majority — for it is safek d'oraisa to be strict; even second — if it were d'oraisa; if so no distinction between issur and tumah; if so from Bekhorot 22 for R' David from nevelah in shechutah and more difficulties — if it were that returns-and-stirs to impart tumah is difficult what relevance to R' David who holds returns-and-stirs to reject from Mishnah that with majority we say returns-and-stirs; if so at least tumah exists that was not entirely nullified — behold nevelah piece nullified in two shechutah and afterward two nevelah pieces fell — returns-and-stirs — learn tumah is like one who exists; and why would it nullify in the majority; however this difficulty to R' David — rather certainly since it is d'rabbanan one cannot compare decrees to each other; and one may say in issur they decreed in tumah they did not decree; and he needed to challenge R' David who says so in tumah; and what Pri Chadash challenged in Raavad's reason — what relevance in terumah that raising is required — behold it does not medameh at all; and he expanded there is no reason for this — for medameh is always by calculation — behold se'ah that rose always has a hundred parts non-sacred and a hundred parts terumah disagreeing non-sacred and they nullify it; and also this is not difficult for Tosafot who forbade it to non-priests — learn they established non-sacred should not nullify it — for otherwise why forbid to non-priests; and so explained in Yerushalmi there on that Mishnah se'ah terumah fell to a hundred of non-sacred — all nullify it; and challenges our Mishnah disagrees with R' Yosi who says by calculation; and answers R' Yosi says it does not medameh at all; rather he said and it is that non-sacred increased on terumah's account the measure to be nullified in the majority — behold at least terumah must be nullified and those hundred parts do not combine: however in Rashi who explained permitted the se'ah to non-priests and he is only liable to pay money to the kohen — nevertheless Rashi's view is like Rambam and Raavad we do not say returns-and-stirs as Tosafot wrote explicitly; difficult from that terumah; and for his approach the explanation does not apply since lacking raising — one must say Rashi holds our Gemara disagrees with Yerushalmi explaining R' Shimon's reason knowledge sanctifies it and all that stands to be raised, etc.; for so is the Gemara's view in Shabbat daf 142 — R' Yosi who holds medameh on Shabbat holds like R' Shimon it is like moram and is like fixing; and the Gemara rejects forever not like moram — only holds already nullified and became non-sacred and does not return and stir even not like moram; and so Gemara's words in Avodah Zarah lean: in truth on Rambam there appears wonder — since he ruled like R' Shimon investigate with known from that he holds taste because like moram 20 — why did he not rule like R' Yosi medameh on Shabbat — behold like moram; and he explained the Mishnah ruled explicitly not like R' Yosi; and so appears from his book he omitted that of R' Yosi; and one must say he holds even though in seif 4 it depends on R' Yosi's matter on R' Shimon; but for conclusion we say R' Yosi's words can give eye on one side and eat on other do not come from R' Yosi like R' Shimon — for even regarding returns-and-stirs it is like moram; but regarding eating behold forbidden to eat until raised; therefore forbidden to raise on Shabbat since fixing: and what he challenged on Rambam who holds all that is in noten ta'am whether not its species or its species requires sixty — we do not say already nullified returns-and-stirs; but in mashahu not returns-and-stirs — and difficult — if so that which teaches se'ah terumah fell to a hundred, raised and another fell — behold there is noten ta'am; and his words unclear — plainly in raising we say no issur exists; and behold such are Rosh's words who ruled we say returns-and-stirs; therefore R' Shimon knowledge permits it — all that stands to be raised like moram; and in explanation we say in Yerushalmi according to the Sages raising permits it; and to Pri Chadash what relevance in raising — behold returns-and-stirs; or since raised no longer say returns-and-stirs; and in particular I wrote all d'rabbanan; and with this Tosafot's difficulty in chapter Ha'Orlah is resolved — they challenged according to R' Eliezer who holds since can give terumah to kohen it is davar sheyeish lo matirin — if so a hundred why terumah stands in one hundred — see there; and one may say certainly this is explained in the Gemara in Bekhorot if we say issur is like one who exists we say returns-and-stirs; but if not like one who exists we do not say returns-and-stirs; and behold that which we say davar sheyeish lo matirin not nullified until you eat them in issur eat b'heter — that is if issur like one who exists well one may say what need rely on nullification; but if issur like it does not exist and issur entirely departed — if so not relevant until you eat them; and this is correct in reasoning; and accordingly in terumah we hold raising is required — in this it does not return-and-stir at all even in less than sixty — the Sages established to say there is no issur; and if so since no returns-and-stirs issur departed even davar sheyeish lo matirin not relevant; therefore only in lechem panim that melted where they did not enact raising; and similarly one may say according to R' Eliezer; and this is correct; and that which the Gemara challenges in Nedarim regarding terumah davar sheyeish lo matirin can ask — in truth can change; but truth of matter answers even where raising not relevant nevertheless not relevant davar sheyeish lo matirin since not a mitzvah — investigate for this is clear: and clear according to Ramban in its species in sixty returns-and-stirs — that is wet in wet; but in dry one-in-two nullified and we do not say against not its species; and if so from where to say in this until sixty returns-and-stirs — what forced that sixty so and against not its species we do not decree in dry; and if so that which Ramban explained in Orlah orlah came up orlah because more than sixty because stam teaches even wet in wet wine in wine — they decreed against not its species; and one must say conversely in terumah that teaches stam according to the Sages raising required even in dry for reason nullified raising and not because less than sixty: however in Rosh chapter 5 of Hullin there is doubt — he brought proof from that terumah that raising or fit for raising required for R' Shimon like moram — what his view forever we say returns-and-stirs or only in sixty; and Pri Chadash was uncertain and did not know his uncertainty — behold in Avodah Zarah stam Rosh in wine fell to cistern until sixty returns-and-stirs and no more; and matter explained he holds for halachah like Rabbeinu Tam and Ramban; and one must say he holds that se'ah of terumah deals in wet not dry; and with this it resolves that for conclusion came up that which the Sages require raising because unlike them returns-and-stirs; but not that raising prevents — it is only theft of owner; and from cistern he brought proof distinction between knowledge and without knowledge — heard from the Sages until here he holds according to the Sages in terumah raising required; but where raising not required like where returns-and-stirs not relevant such as in Orlah that estimates in two hundred even the Sages agree nevertheless knowledge required — for R' Shimon all that stands to be raised like moram nevertheless requires knowledge; and Rosh does not hold like Rambam we do not say like moram only after knowledge — only holds stam; and if so why does R' Shimon require knowledge — rather the measure to nullify issur; and if so in Orlah orlah came up orlah nevertheless for R' Shimon knowledge required; and if so even according to the Sages knowledge required — in Orlah the Sages do not disagree with R' Shimon to be lenient; and learn issur does not return to heter except after knowledge; and this is Rosh's intent correctly and does not contradict his words at all; and now since Rosh and Ramban and Ran and Rashba in one approach — Heaven forbid to disagree with their words in any way; and Pri Chadash invented from his heart to be lenient in wet until majority and I do not know why not until half which is also Torah issur and safek asham talui; and if the Mishnah forced him that teaches until majority; and for this we should not say against Torah view; but clear as he wrote second raising then for all it does not return-and-stir; and therefore all he wrote at length only to permit; and the law is true law as Rama wrote everywhere we say returns-and-stirs; and even known — for what helps knowledge that is in a matter that has sixty; but in a matter without sixty knowledge does not help; and that Rosh wrote in Laws of Yayin Nesech stam until sixty first-first nullified — one must say without knowledge and stam as explanation; and requires study below in Laws of Yayin Nesech siman 100 at least no doubt at all in Rama's words — mark well do not move:"
  },
  "pitchei-teshuva": {
    "1#_": "Issur bones combine. Shach ruled like Ran that soft bones combine with issur; and see in responsum Mahari Levi siman 28 who did not rule so, only that they combine with heter — see there his proof; and see there further in siman 27 who wrote there is room to doubt whether specifically in cooking issur bones combine with heter and not in salting or otherwise; therefore in weak issur oil by law does not require sixty except by stringency comparing to cooking they combine in any event; but oil issur requiring sixty because issur spreads — perhaps it does not spread in bones and does not combine even of heter — see there; and see in Sefer Teivat Gimma parashat Vayera sign 9 who wrote even eiver min hachai that fell to heter — bones in it combine with heter even though in eiver min hachai bone combines to kezayit — it is a novelty — investigate with your eyes regarding its emission — see there:",
    "2#_": "In gravy. See in responsum Beit Yaakov end siman 57 who wrote that what rises as steam to the top is lacking from the food; and when estimating in sixty one must estimate what is lacking through the steam — see there:",
    "3#_": "One does not nullify issur. Shach wrote in the name of poskim that it is d'rabbanan; and see in responsum Noda Biyehudah second edition part 14 siman 45 who wrote specifically to nullify the taste of issur is d'rabbanan but substance of issur dry in dry in the majority is from d'oraisa; and the same applies to nullify in sixty — see there (and with this Tosafot Yisrael's difficulty on Mishnayot Maaser Sheni chapter 2 mishnah 4 is resolved — see there; and see in Netziv what I wrote on this); but wet in wet to nullify in sixty it appears there permitted from d'oraisa; and see on this in responsum Pnei Aryeh 62 at length; and see in Noda Biyehudah first edition part 14 siman 26 s.v. and in any event I say; and see in responsum Shemen Rokeach who disagrees part 2 siman 14 who expanded greatly on this matter and explained many details in this law:",
    "4#_": "L'chatchila. [Avodat HaTaharah in the name of Taz that permitted if his intent is not specifically when impossible otherwise; and in Pri Megadim explained even if possible only that it is trouble, also permitted — see there; and see in Sefer Beit Yehudah who wrote accordingly it appears regarding fruit tree called melons that they cook in water and afterward squeeze the melons and discard them and strain the sack and cook it in honey to dip bread and meat — need not check each melon for worms because commonly in them; after there is much trouble to check all — however Pri Megadim's words are not compelling — see there] and see in responsum Noda Biyehudah part 14 siman 26 regarding krupnik called hozin without zayin — it is bladder of impure fish dried and placed in honey liquid and its nature whitens the liquid and the great ones of the generation forbade on this since it remains in the liquid and is like cooked; and if because nullified in sixty — one may not nullify issur l'chatchila — and he expanded on this and it did not occur to him it is full heter for many reasons — see there; and see in responsum Tiferet Tzvi part 14 siman 73:",
    "5#_": "For his sake. Avodat HaTaholah in the name of Taz who says permitted — he is shogeg; and see in responsum Tzemach Tzedek siman 464 who wrote if he is uncertain in hora'ah and asked students and did not ask famous decisors — he is not called shogeg — see there; and with this Beit Yaakov responsum siman 85 who wanted to compare if done on surface of another woman's deed to done by sage's instruction — how are they different — see there; and see further in Beit Yaakov there who disagrees with Taz on this. [And see Magen Avraham siman 318 s.k. 3; and see in responsum Chasam Sofer siman 88 what he wrote on this; and it appears his words agree with Taz — see there]:",
    "6#_": "But if it became known. Shach disagrees on this — one may not distinguish at all between known or not, only between shogeg and mezid; and see further in Shach who expanded to refute Issur Veheter's view who wrote even where we say chein nafsho there is also distinction between known or not — it does not exist; and he concluded even Rama in d'mem concluded and Turei Chayyim brought Issur Veheter's words that without knowledge we do not say chein nafsho lest the Rav retracted and therefore did not write this law in his hagahot — see there; and so concluded Pri Chadash and Minchat Yitzchak general chapter 5 s.k. 38 — except Minchat Yitzchak wrote there that in wet in wet since in Peirush HaMishnayot we do not say chein nafsho there is room to be lenient in this without knowledge — see there; and Pri Megadim wrote and for halachah without knowledge in meat in milk certainly forbidden d'oraisa; and in other issurim in Peirush HaMishnayot I have no authority to be lenient — see there; and it appears even to be strict is not clear to him; and see in responsum Rabbeinu Akiva Eiger part 70 end seif s.k. 7 who wondered on Shach in what he wrote perhaps the Rav retracted for certainly no doubt the Rav certainly retracted for behold above siman 73 seif 66 is found liver stuck who wrote in hagahah chein nafsho and requires sixty against entire fowl; and language of is found it appears they did not notice liver remained in fowl until afterward is found so — behold even without knowledge we say chein nafsho; and so we rule siman side 2 seif 3 regarding heart; and since so even Pri Megadim wrote not clear to him to be lenient in Peirush HaMishnayot — to be strict is clear to me for behold in that of heart and liver no lenient view mentioned in Peirush HaMishnayot; and again wrote to resolve Shach's words — since Shach wrote plainly that piece certainly forbidden even without knowledge lest much issur remained in it since so automatically others forbidden if no sixty against entire piece — though we say without knowledge we do not say chein nafsho nevertheless after mixture became known and piece stands in its issur chein nafsho forbids now the others — as if fell to another pot it would forbid so too forbids pieces in this pot; and if so one may say the reason in heart and liver requires sixty against entire fowl deals with is found stuck while pot still boiling; and since fowl remained in its issur now after knowledge fowl became nevelah and forbids others as if now fell to another pot; but where not relevant such as removed fowl from pot — known mixture or pot cooled — known mixture in this Shach was uncertain if Rama retracted or not; and with this Pri Megadim wrote not clear to him to be lenient in Peirush HaMishnayom even to be strict not clear — therefore one who is lenient in Peirush HaMishnayom or Shabbat need one does not rebuke — see there:"
  },
  "rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd": {
    "1#_": "(Siman 99 seif 4) \"This applies in not its species.\" From Beit Yosef's words it appears Rashba deals with without seeing the issur's quality at first; and for this reason in mashahu not its species forbidden — for we say also the issur was large and diminished and nevertheless in mashahu we are lenient like Taz and Shach; and however it appears to me in my weak view to learn from Rashba's words in Teshuvot HaRamban — without seeing issur at first forbidden also in mashahu except if we saw issur at first and there was sixty and heter greatly diminished and issur did not diminish much — in mashahu we estimate the absorbed with good estimate and it helps nullify; and in mashahu forbidden since now there is not sixty; and accordingly Tur wrote well and Rosh disagrees who also in mashahu permitted where we saw issur at first and there was sixty; and Beit Yosef's difficulty removed as appears to me for practical halachah:",
    "4#_": "(There) And likewise for one for whom it was nullified. And if he nullified stam for whoever wants to buy — behold it is like done specifically for them and forbidden to all — responsum Rivash siman 495; and we hear from this even one for whom it was nullified did not know of it — not like Taz (s.k. 10):",
    "5#_": "(Shach s.k. 15) And perhaps the Rav also retracted. It appears to me there is no doubt in this — certainly so — for the Rav retracted; for behold above (siman 73 seif 66) is found liver in fowl, etc.; and wrote came up in hagahah; and therefore if liver whole and stuck to fowl fowl became chein nafsho and requires sixty among other things against fowl; and is found certainly it appears without known mixture — for liver stuck to it; and even so all forbidden; and even law of davuk we say in it from kli sheni — if cooked and there was not sixty at first — we say law of piece itself chein nafsho; and in truth I saw Turei Chayyim (general chapter 5 law 5) one who brought Issur Veheter's words that even mixed first and without knowledge heter added afterward — permitted; and wrote came up; and these are his words requires study — for if so why forbidden issur davuk as explained above regarding liver; however one may say in issur the clinging does not combine — end of his words; and I did not merit to understand — for behold in davuk the taste quickly absorbed from known mixture or lest it was once outside; and if so from kli sheni in mixed first in wet and there was not sixty — certainly absorbed entered first known mixture; and Hashem will enlighten my eyes:",
    "6#_": "(Seif 6) But if it fell by itself. Alei Derech wrote specifically fell by itself; but if he threw b'shogeg forbidden to add and nullify it — see there in siman 677:"
  },
  "siftei-kohen": {
    "1#א": "\"Issur bones, etc.\" And for practical halachah in Talmud and Yerushalmi like Ra'ah's view in sixty in Beit HaYayin (daf 96b) and Ran chapter 5 of Hullin — soft bones combine with issur because of marrow within them and because of their softness moisture emerges; but dry bones or egg shells and the like — even those of issur combine to nullify issur; and so Issur Veheter's view and Bach's view:",
    "1#ג": "\"For such is essential.\" Thus Bach; and one should wonder — he should have ruled issur bones combine with issur, for since they are stuck to meat they first received issur taste and chein nafsho; and he holds (above siman 98 seif 5) even in a matter with no taste by itself we say chein nafsho — there is no wonder; for apparently the Rav did not write above siman 98 seif 5 to be concerned for stringency in new earthenware vessel chein nafsho even though it has no taste by itself — only because it has no remedy through hagalah and cannot remove the issur as written there; and if so bone vessel that has remedy through hagalah as explained in Orach Chayyim siman 451 — certainly comparable to metal vessel the Rav holds we do not say chein nafsho unless old when what was absorbed in it chein nafsho:",
    "3#א": "\"The absorbed piece, etc.\" Explanation: the piece absorbed from issur that fell to heter and there is not sixty in heter to nullify issur except through combining the piece — the piece too combines to nullify; however the piece itself is forbidden as Mechaber wrote end of siman 106; but we do not practice so — rather sixty against entire piece and piece itself forbidden:",
    "4#_": "Even though there was more heter at first and it diminished in cooking and was absorbed in pot — even so we estimate only as it appears before us; that also regarding issur there was more and diminished; and if we know issur measure we also estimate what was absorbed:",
    "5#א": "\"One does not nullify issur l'chatchila.\" Rashba wrote in Teshuvot HaRamban part 4 beginning gate 3; and Ran chapter 5 of Hullin — this is d'rabbanan according to Tosafot; but according to Raavad and Rambam and Ramban and Rashba and Tur and Beit Yosef and Hagahot Maimoniyot and Semag and Hagahot Or Zarua and Mordechai and Ran and Hagahot ShaDa — d'oraisa:",
    "5#ג": "\"If b'shogeg permitted.\" And this is specifically in other issurim according to Taz who do not say chein nafsho in other issurim; but in basar b'chalav chein nafsho — even b'shogeg forbidden if afterward heter added:",
    "5#ד": "\"Forbidden for the nullifier himself, etc.\" And like Beit Yosef; and it appears if it was not his and he also did not intend to nullify for himself — permitted for him since you did not permit one for whom he intended to nullify; and forbidden to sell also to Israel lest they benefit from what was nullified (Aruch general 24):",
    "5#ח": "\"But a piece that absorbed issur, etc.\" — for we say chein nafsho. At first glance difficult — what relevance to chein nafsho; for even according to the one who says we do not say chein nafsho in other issurim — nevertheless piece that absorbed issur and heter added afterward — the piece itself remained in issur as all poskim wrote end of siman 110 that issur in it is not entirely expelled; rather the Rav comes to inform us piece absorbed issur and heter added afterward — even addition forbidden because no sixty against entire piece; but if he increased b'shogeg against all that became nevelah even in basar b'chalav wet in wet all permitted; but piece that absorbed issur even if he increased sixty against entire piece piece itself remained in issur:",
    "5#ט": "\"And some say even where, etc.\" Issur Veheter general 24 dalet 10 wrote explicitly even where we say chein nafsho such as wet in wet or wet in dry or dry in wet — there is distinction between known and not known; and if not known we do not say chein nafsho lest Rav retracted and therefore Rama omitted this from his hagahot; and so Pri Chadash and Minchat Yitzchak:",
    "5#י": "\"And accordingly, etc.\" And we did not practice so. Meaning accordingly since addition does not help — if according to the one who says wet in wet chein nafsho or piece absorbed issur for all; or mixture became known — teacher must investigate when heter was added (Aruch general 37); and we did not practice so:",
    "6#א": "\"A d'rabbanan issur, etc.\" In Sifrei I expanded in explanation — d'rabbanan issur that has essence in d'oraisa; and per Rashba's and Tur's and Beit Yosef's explanation; and per Hagahot Maimoniyot and Semag and Hagahot Or Zarua and Mordechai and Ran and Hagahot ShaDa:",
    "6#ד": "\"Returns and stirs and becomes forbidden.\" Specifically in one issur species; but in two issur species that differ in taste each nullifies its fellow's taste; and as explained end of siman 98:",
    "6#ה": "\"It makes no difference known in between or not.\" So Turei Chayyim general chapter 5 dalet 10 in the name of Maharai in Hagahot ShaDa; but in truth it appears he did not write there Maharai (in Hagahot ShaDa siman 59) — investigate:",
    "6#ו": "\"And afterward it fell from the water, etc.\" And Turei Chayyim general chapter 5 dalet 11 wrote even l'chatchila permitted to put water in meat pot since already nullified; and one should not challenge from Taz and Shach above siman 94 seif 6:"
  },
  "turei-zahav": {
    "1#א": "Issur bones combine. Because they are not edible they are not in the prohibition; nevertheless they absorb through cooking — therefore combine to nullify issur because issur spreads into them and there is moisture in them to mix issur absorbed from forbidden meat.",
    "7#_": "\"If a small amount of issur was absorbed, etc.\" Rashba in Teshuvot HaRamban daf 126 learned this law and these are his words: from what is in chapter One May Not Leave Standing jugs that a non-Jew puts wine in them — if its use is to pour abundantly each day even ben yomo permitted l'chatchila; and if its use is occasionally like a bowl — forbidden even in abundance; decree lest use small amount:",
    "1#ב": "\"And the body of the pot, etc.\" Meaning that pot in which one cooks now issur and heter; but a plain pot already forbidden and afterward cooks heter — body of pot certainly combines with issur.",
    "4#ב": "Even though there was more heter, etc. Rashba in his novellae and Ran wrote — if we saw issur at time of fall and it did not diminish by one measure with heter — we estimate the absorbed with good estimate; and some say we do not estimate absorbed of pot for perhaps contracted and returned and forbids; and as Maharshal and Taz:",
    "4#ג": "\"And this applies in not its species.\" In this sixty from Torah is required since noten ta'am — therefore cannot estimate absorbed; and some say why do we permit now when sixty before us — perhaps much heter was lost through cooking:",
    "5#א": "\"One does not nullify issur l'chatchila, etc.\" In siman 84 seif 13 explained if intent only for something else such as fixing honey — permitted; and siman 137 seif 2 I wrote specifically if impossible otherwise:",
    "5#ב": "\"If b'shogeg permitted.\" Tur brought this in this language — we hold all issurim increased upon them b'shogeg permitted; and Beit Yosef brought source — dispute of R' Meir and R' Yosi Gittin 54:",
    "5#ד": "\"And likewise for one for whom it was nullified.\" Rashal explained there in chapter 5 of Hullin — specifically when this one knew it was nullified for him and it pleases him even if he did not command to nullify; but if he did not know from him — permitted for him since like shogeg:",
    "5#ה": "\"And we did not practice so.\" And this is Tur's reason who disagrees with Semag who wrote must ask if heter was added afterward; and Tur wrote need not, etc.; and Rashal wrote Semag's reason lest did not know there is issur in matter and added much to pot because afterward will warn himself and tell teacher such happened:"
  },
  "yad-avraham": {
    "1#_": "(Siman 99 seif 1) Piece of nevelah with meat and bones that fell to pot — bones in it combine to nullify issur; so Rosh chapter 5 of Terumot and Rosh chapter 5 of Hullin and Tur because we are concerned lest issur diminished and absorbed in bones; and if we saw issur while raw certainly sixty suffices against meat seen while piece was raw; and Bach explicitly; and even according to Rama who holds chein nafsho in other issurim and in matter without taste — nevertheless here he agrees as Shach wrote s.k. 3 bone like metal vessel; and if cooked first with bones too halachah thus bones combine with heter if they knew meat measure at first:",
    "2#_": "(Seif 2) In what case stated when fell while raw; but if cooked first bones combine with issur because absorbed from nevelah meat; it appears also for heter bones combine if they knew meat measure at first:",
    "3#_": "(There) But if cooked first bones combine with issur because absorbed from nevelah meat when cooked alone. Later poskim wrote likewise if nevelah was salted first; and if afterward fell to heter while raw — bones combine with issur because absorbed when salted alone:",
    "4#_": "(Seif 4) We estimate heter and issur as they appear. Even though more heter at first and diminished in cooking and absorbed in pot — because also from issur was more and diminished; and if we know issur measure we estimate also what pot absorbed:",
    "5#_": "(Seif 5) One does not nullify issur l'chatchila. Noda Biyehudah second edition part 14 siman 45 wrote even those who say permitted to nullify issur l'chatchila when impossible otherwise — only b'dieved; but l'chatchila all forbid:",
    "6#_": "(There Taz s.k. 9) If he thinks permitted to increase in issur — called shogeg; erring in law called shogeg not ones; even regarding oath liable korban as Mechaber wrote in Choshen Mishpat:",
    "7#_": "(Seif 6) D'rabbanan issur one does not mix by hand. [See Ran chapter 3 of Pesachim regarding how one separates]; but if fell by itself and not enough heter to nullify — one increases and nullifies:"
  },
  "yad-ephraim": {
    "1#_": "One does not nullify. See Avodat HaTaharah; and She'elat Yaavetz part 2 siman 131 wrote if one wants to make issur improved-spoiled in order to mix in heter — also included in prohibition of nullifying issur l'chatchila:"
  }
};
